1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,095
Array methods on arrays of objects. On the older exams this was

2
00:00:04,195 --> 00:00:08,491
a whole problem, around 38 points, and the same methods still show

3
00:00:08,591 --> 00:00:13,220
up in other problems. We'll also cover destructuring, spread, and JSON.

4
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:18,193
Our data is an array of employee objects,

5
00:00:18,293 --> 00:00:20,384
each with a name and a salary.

6
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:23,623
forEach runs a function for every item. It returns

7
00:00:23,723 --> 00:00:27,206
undefined, so it's only for side effects, like logging.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,119
find returns the first item that matches, the

9
00:00:30,219 --> 00:00:33,104
whole object, or undefined if nothing matches.

10
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,398
findIndex returns the index of the

11
00:00:35,498 --> 00:00:37,900
first match instead, or negative one.

12
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,708
every asks whether all items pass the test.

13
00:00:40,808 --> 00:00:43,060
some asks whether at least one does.

14
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:48,835
The three names, then Mia's whole object, then undefined because nobody earns

15
00:00:48,935 --> 00:00:52,765
over 99 thousand, then 2 and negative 1, and true true.

16
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:59,336
map builds a new array of the same length, here just the

17
00:00:59,436 --> 00:01:03,197
names, and join glues them into one string with a separator.

18
00:01:03,266 --> 00:01:06,243
reduce is the one to really practice. It carries an

19
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:10,648
accumulator through the array. Whatever your callback returns becomes the

20
00:01:10,748 --> 00:01:14,087
accumulator for the next item. For a maximum value, start

21
00:01:14,187 --> 00:01:16,198
at zero and keep the bigger salary.

22
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:19,246
For the maximum object, leave out the initial value.

23
00:01:19,346 --> 00:01:22,444
Then the accumulator starts as the first employee, and

24
00:01:22,544 --> 00:01:25,286
you keep whichever object has the higher salary.

25
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:29,730
And a sum is the classic: start at zero and add each salary.

26
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:38,016
Mia, Ben, Ava. Then 95000, Ben's whole object, and the total, 228000.

27
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,320
Sorting. The exam likes a boolean parameter that picks the sort order

28
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:49,327
or the sort key. The comparator returns a negative number to put

29
00:01:49,427 --> 00:01:52,208
a first, and a positive number to put b first.

30
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:57,712
For strings, use localeCompare. For numbers, just subtract: a's salary minus

31
00:01:57,812 --> 00:02:01,992
b's salary sorts ascending, and b minus a sorts descending.

32
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:03,848
Now we sort by name, then by

33
00:02:03,948 --> 00:02:06,670
salary, and then print the original array.

34
00:02:06,766 --> 00:02:10,074
And finally, numbers with and without a comparator.

35
00:02:10,466 --> 00:02:14,262
By name: Ava, Ben, Mia. By salary: Ava, Mia, Ben.

36
00:02:14,362 --> 00:02:19,271
And look, the original array changed too. Without a comparator,

37
00:02:19,371 --> 00:02:23,326
the numbers sort as strings, so 100 comes before 9.

38
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:27,949
Exam trap. sort changes the original array, and without

39
00:02:28,049 --> 00:02:31,068
a comparator it compares the values as strings.

40
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:38,434
Quick return values. push returns the new length, not the array.

41
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:40,545
pop returns the item it removed.

42
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:42,909
And reverse flips the array in place.

43
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:49,032
4, then 4 again, the removed item, and then 3, 2, 1.

44
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:55,138
Array destructuring pulls items out by position.

45
00:02:55,238 --> 00:02:57,688
An empty spot between commas skips one.

46
00:02:57,766 --> 00:03:01,790
Object destructuring pulls properties out by name. salary

47
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:04,684
colon pay renames it, and dept equals IT

48
00:03:04,784 --> 00:03:07,722
gives a default because first has no dept.

49
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:09,893
Three dots on the left side is rest.

50
00:03:09,993 --> 00:03:12,452
It collects whatever's left into an array.

51
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:15,464
Three dots on the right side, or in a call,

52
00:03:15,564 --> 00:03:19,129
is spread. It expands an array into separate values.

53
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,141
Spread works on objects too. This copies first

54
00:03:22,241 --> 00:03:25,844
and overrides the salary, without changing the original.

55
00:03:26,233 --> 00:03:31,893
Mia, 72000, and IT. Then 5 and the rest, 6 7 8. Max is 8, and

56
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:39,165
the new array ends in 9. And the copy has 80000, while first still has 72000.

57
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:43,682
Exam trap. It's the same three dots. On the left of

58
00:03:43,782 --> 00:03:47,371
an equals sign, or in a parameter list, it's rest. On

59
00:03:47,471 --> 00:03:50,573
the right, or in a function call, it's spread.

60
00:03:53,666 --> 00:03:57,003
JSON dot stringify turns an object into a string. The

61
00:03:57,103 --> 00:04:00,958
keys get double quotes, and functions are dropped completely.

62
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:03,157
JSON dot parse goes the other way,

63
00:04:03,257 --> 00:04:05,709
turning the string back into an object.

64
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:08,642
The string has no hi method, its type is

65
00:04:08,742 --> 00:04:11,352
string, and parsing it back gives us Mia.

66
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:18,514
Practice question 1. products is an array of objects with an item

67
00:04:18,614 --> 00:04:23,724
and a price. Write getMostExpensive using reduce, returning the whole object. And

68
00:04:23,824 --> 00:04:28,612
write sortProducts, which sorts by price, ascending when the boolean is true

69
00:04:28,712 --> 00:04:32,021
and descending otherwise. Pause the video and try it.

70
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:44,611
For the max, reduce with no initial value and keep whichever object

71
00:04:44,711 --> 00:04:48,693
has the higher price. For sorting, use the boolean to pick the

72
00:04:48,793 --> 00:04:52,776
comparator: a minus b for ascending, b minus a for descending.

73
00:04:56,866 --> 00:05:01,086
Practice question 2. What does this print? Pause the video and try it.

74
00:05:09,366 --> 00:05:14,415
First, filter keeps the pen and the mug, map uppercases them, and join

75
00:05:14,515 --> 00:05:19,344
gives PEN slash MUG. Next, sort returns the same array, not a copy,

76
00:05:19,444 --> 00:05:23,757
so pushing 9 onto r also changes q, and q triple-equals r is

77
00:05:23,857 --> 00:05:29,642
true. Finally, item is lamp, and rest collects the remaining property, price 30.

78
00:05:33,733 --> 00:05:38,399
Recap. find gives an item or undefined, findIndex gives an index or

79
00:05:38,499 --> 00:05:42,880
negative one, and every and some give booleans. map makes a new

80
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:47,503
array, and reduce handles max and sum. sort changes the array, so

81
00:05:47,603 --> 00:05:53,691
use subtraction for numbers and localeCompare for strings. And know your destructuring,

82
00:05:53,791 --> 00:05:57,177
rest versus spread, and JSON stringify and parse.

